WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.240
The first film showed growth in length near the tips.

2
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.120
So drive a nail into a tree trunk, 1 m up.

3
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.880
How high is it many years later?

4
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:20.993
It is still at the same height.

5
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:24.613
That part of the trunk stopped lengthening long ago.

6
00:00:25.893 --> 00:00:28.653
A tree gets taller only in young shoots near its tips.

7
00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:31.393
But the trunk keeps getting wider.

8
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:37.777
Width comes from a thin cylinder of dividing cells inside the stem, the vascular

9
00:00:37.777 --> 00:00:41.617
cambium. It lies between the xylem and the phloem.

10
00:00:42.817 --> 00:00:46.657
It is a lateral meristem, running along older stems and roots.

11
00:00:47.717 --> 00:00:51.117
Cut a thin strip from the centre of the trunk out to its surface.

12
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:55.897
The vascular cambium is one thin teal line inside it.

13
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.300
When a cambium cell divides, some daughters are pushed inward.

14
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.120
They become secondary xylem, which is wood.

15
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:06.943
Other daughters are pushed outward.

16
00:01:08.203 --> 00:01:13.203
They become secondary phloem, the tissue that carries sugars from the leaves to the

17
00:01:13.203 --> 00:01:14.203
rest of the plant.

18
00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:19.627
In a temperate oak, the cambium works in spring and summer and rests in winter.

19
00:01:21.087 --> 00:01:22.467
Spring wood has large vessels.

20
00:01:23.927 --> 00:01:25.587
Late summer wood has smaller ones.

21
00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:31.590
Where one summer's dense wood meets the next spring's large vessels, a line appears.

22
00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.830
Each year usually leaves one ring.

23
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:39.603
As the trunk widens, its old skin, the epidermis, splits.

24
00:01:40.863 --> 00:01:45.963
A second lateral meristem, the cork cambium, usually forms just beneath it.

25
00:01:47.123 --> 00:01:50.083
Outward it makes cork, whose cells have waterproofed walls.

26
00:01:51.523 --> 00:01:54.243
Inward it makes a thin living layer, the phelloderm.

27
00:01:55.703 --> 00:01:59.223
Cork, cork cambium and phelloderm together form the periderm.

28
00:02:00.163 --> 00:02:02.563
The periderm replaces the epidermis.

29
00:02:02.563 --> 00:02:03.563
... ...

30
00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:06.717
Bark is everything outside the vascular cambium.

31
00:02:07.897 --> 00:02:11.297
So its inner part is living phloem, not only dead cork.

32
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.250
This is the first film's rule, turned sideways.

33
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.450
Over one year, count the new wood cells in a row out from the cambium.

34
00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.550
Multiply by their average width.

35
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.910
Cell widths change through the season, so the average matters.

36
00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.770
That gives the width of the ring.

37
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:34.793
Maize has no conventional vascular cambium.

38
00:02:36.053 --> 00:02:38.533
Its bundles are scattered, and it makes no wood.

39
00:02:39.793 --> 00:02:44.673
It grew tall from its shoot tip and from meristems at the bases of its leaves and

40
00:02:44.673 --> 00:02:45.673
internodes.

41
00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:48.207
So a tree's shoot tips add height.

42
00:02:49.427 --> 00:02:51.967
Its vascular cambium adds width and wood.
